Turn dimensions into independent and testable constraints (@engineering-dimension-constraints: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/practice/Dimensions-and-tolerances.lingua#r_0K38T1TBRR4N9ESP1FYR55H90T") { r_05128EACECEA90BEE05B27AD39
A dimension connects specified geometric features under a declared convention. Before reading its number, identify its endpoints or associated features, direction, units and reference conditions. Distinguish locating a feature from specifying its size. A datum feature on a real part helps establish a reference; it is not a perfectly known mathematical plane merely because a drawing labels it. The common model below begins with ideal parallel planes. Later form and datum realization require the detailed drawing and metrology formation.

For the tray, name A as the internal longitudinal span, B and C as the two insert lengths, and g as the remaining longitudinal gap when the inserts meet end to end at the left reference plane. The model is A=B+C+g. It assumes aligned ideal lengths, contact without an unmodeled intervening layer, and a common set of stated conditions. It does not derive from how wide the sketch looks. With A=50, B=20 and C=29 mm, g=1 mm. Four exact nominal values are consistent only if they satisfy this relation. Adding g=1.2 mm to these three values creates a contradiction, with closure residual −0.2 mm.

Reuse [Solve simultaneous constraints without losing their solution set](../../mathematics/core/Solve-simultaneous-constraints-without-losing-their-solution-set.lingua#r_03FC1F97250326F9B36A1B2F6B): equations can supply new information, repeat information, or conflict. The single closure equation by itself leaves several dimensions free. Choosing A, B and C fixes g within this ideal model; choosing only A and B leaves many possible C/g pairs. A redundant dimension can be useful as an explicitly identified reference, but treating all closed-chain dimensions as independently assignable can produce inconsistent requirements. Actual drawing conventions govern how reference and controlled dimensions are marked; this lesson is an algebraic explanation, not a current standards specification.

Position dimensions and chain dimensions also carry different dependence. The nominal reference positions are x0=0, x1=B, x2=B+C and x3=A. Then B=x1−x0, C=x2−x1 and g=x3−x2. A common translation of all positions cancels from every distance. Setting x2 and B and C independently without enforcing x2=B+C would describe a different joint constraint set. Carry the defining relations into any later tolerance or uncertainty calculation.

The same discipline of naming quantities applies beyond geometry. In [Choose what a model must represent before calculating](Choose-what-a-model-must-represent-before-calculating.lingua#r_0F7FD805EB7379B6E978BD4F6C), counted stock equals initial stock plus admitted inputs minus removals under its boundary assumptions. That is a count balance, whereas A=B+C+g is a geometrical chain. A flow rate cannot be added directly to a stored volume without a time account, and a tolerance cannot be added to an unrelated force just because both have numerical values. Shared algebra does not make different physical models interchangeable.

**Work:** draw the chain with four labeled positions, then provide two different independent sets of three lengths that reconstruct it. Explain the unresolved freedom when one is omitted and the contradiction when an inconsistent fourth is imposed. Convert the complete account from mm to m and recover the same physical gap. Read the Dimensioning unit through [Basic Blueprint Reading — Costin and Linn-Benton](../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/sources/Blueprint-Reading.lingua#r_3BSMCZ60NQ4ESQ5YK6K2JAB7F5) for drawing communication, and reuse [Units, dimensions, and equations that can make sense](../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/foundations/Units-and-dimensions.lingua#r_3A511KGNX59K2YHKT83NM38NE1) for quantity equations. The next shared lesson separates nominal values, limits and observation.

**Continue:** [Dimensions, tolerances, references, and an inspection question](../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/practice/Dimensions-and-tolerances.lingua#r_0K38T1TBRR4N9ESP1FYR55H90T).

**Return:** [Engineering](../Engineering.lingua#r_5DA6VY09MAD2N8D9SH0FFJVV68).
